The compiler owns a fixed set of traits for user-defined cast sources.
Conformance proves that a type can reach a target through plain `cast`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING
    value String = cast Label("docs")
]

Fifteen target families support source-authored evidence: `I8`, `I16`, `I32`,
`I64`, `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`, `F16`, `F32`, `F64`, `Bool`, `String`,
`Char` and `Error`. Each target has infallible and fallible evidence. A type
cannot declare both for the same target.

Fallible evidence returns the target and builtin `Error!`. Its caller still
uses plain `cast`; ordinary error handling delivers the actual failure.
`Byte` and `Dec` keep builtin conversion evidence but have no source-authored
traits. See @./#core-cast-traits (Core cast traits) for every signature.
